From: Shadow-MSM contributors
Subject: [PATCH] init: avoid low-RAM trace after successful exec

run_init_process() returning zero means the built-in ELF has been installed
successfully and PID 1 now owns a private userspace page table.  The old
diagnostic ordering nevertheless called shadow_msm_c_trace_hex() before
testing that return value.  Unlike the later diagnostic bridge, this early
helper jumps directly into the identity-mapped resident runtime, so the
success-path call raises a supervisor prefetch abort and kills PID 1 with
SIGSEGV before its first userspace return.

Test success before emitting the failure-only message.  Failed exec attempts
still retain their diagnostic, while successful exec proceeds directly to
ret_from_fork and userspace.

diff --git a/init/main.c b/init/main.c
--- a/init/main.c
+++ b/init/main.c
@@ -1909,12 +1909,12 @@ static int __ref kernel_init(void *unused)
 #endif
 		ret = run_init_process(ramdisk_execute_command);
+		if (!ret)
+			return 0;
 #ifdef CONFIG_SHADOW_MSM_EARLY_TRACE
 		shadow_msm_c_trace_hex(
 			"Shadow-MSM: built-in /init exec failed ",
 			(unsigned long)ret);
 #endif
-		if (!ret)
-			return 0;
 		pr_err("Failed to execute %s (error %d)\n",
 		       ramdisk_execute_command, ret);
 	}
